# Engineering Autopilot

Classify every task silently before implementing: Level 1 (routine), Level 2
(connected), or Level 3 (critical), per `docs/engineering-autopilot.md`.

**Level 1 fast path (no full doc read required):** copy/labels, styling,
isolated component layout, small local UI behavior, fixture updates that do not
alter a data contract, and ordinary explanatory documentation, release notes,
or non-contractual notes. Follow the design system, product voice, and
applicable component rules; inspect the affected component and nearby tests;
run focused lint/type/test validation; check the final diff for unrelated
changes. Do not add architecture documents or broad test suites.

**Level 2 / Level 3 (or ambiguous classification):** read and follow
`docs/engineering-autopilot.md` before planning or implementing. Keep routine
work fast; automatically add architecture, reliability, migration, recovery,
and production-safety checks when the task crosses a Level 3 boundary.

Changes to agent rules, governance documents, product doctrine, design-system
contracts, architecture references, or performance policies are at least Level
2. Treat them as Level 3 when they change an actual security, data, persistence,
scoring, historical-integrity, deployment, or production contract.

Any Supabase, Cloudflare, migration, backfill, persistent-storage, or production
data change must also read and follow `docs/production-data-safety.md` and
`.cursor/rules/production-data-safety.mdc`. Preservation evidence is a release
gate, not an optional rollback note.

Use its document-routing table to load only the canonical references relevant
to the active task. Do not duplicate their long-form guidance into plans or
rules.

This rule does not authorize Codex CLI use. `.cursor/rules/codex-usage.mdc`
remains the authority: Cursor may invoke Codex only when the user explicitly
requests Codex in that turn.

Do not deploy, mutate production data, apply production migrations, commit,
merge, or push without the approvals required by the repository rules.
